<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cosimo de' Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1389">1389</a>–<a href="/years/1464">1464</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Cosimo the Elder · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cosimo de' Medici, called the Elder, was a Florentine banker who controlled the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1434 until his death without holding formal power for long. Head of the Medici Bank, then the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, he governed through allies and manipulated councils and elections. A great patron of Brunelleschi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatello">Donatello</a> and humanist learning, he was honoured after his death as Pater Patriae, father of his country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici.jpg" alt="Cosimo de' Medici" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Bronzino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cosimo_di_Medici_(Bronzino).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1434 <b>Cosimo de’ Medici</b> returned from a year’s exile and, without holding formal power for long, controlled the republic through allies, manipulation of the electoral lists and new councils such as the Council of One Hundred and later the Council of Seventy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cosimo de' Medici</b>, master of Florence from 1434, paid for manuscripts and copyists and placed the books of the collector Niccolò Niccoli in the library of San Marco, opened in the 1440s.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In Florence, after the revolt of the Ciompi wool workers in 1378, an oligarchy of great families led by the Albizzi governed until <b>Cosimo de' Medici</b>’s return in 1434.</blockquote><a href="/government/oligarchy">Oligarchy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
